.TH SHARDPOSTCTL-MIGRATE 1 "2026-09-30" "ShardPost 0.34.0" "ShardPost Manual"
.SH NAME
shardpostctl-migrate \- copy an account from another IMAP server, keeping its identities
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shardpostctl migrate
.BI \-source-addr " host:port"
.BI \-password-file " file"
.RB [ \-master-user
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-sieve-addr
.IR host:port ]
.RI [ options ]
.I account
.br
.B shardpostctl migrate
.RI [ options ]
.BI \-source-user " name"
.BI \-source-namespace " prefix"
.RB [ \-target-prefix
.IR name/ ]
.RB [ \-readers
.IR account,... ]
.B '~public'
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads an account from another IMAP server and writes it into ShardPost
through the import API, keeping what clients care about: UIDVALIDITY,
the UIDs with their gaps, UIDNEXT, flags and keywords, INTERNALDATE and
SAVEDATE, and the MODSEQ of every message. Clients that were synchronized
before the move therefore only fetch what is new, instead of downloading
the whole account again. IMAP APPEND cannot do this, which is why
imapsync and similar tools always produce new UIDs.
.PP
Every run is repeatable. The migrator reads the source, compares it with
the target's inventory and sends only the differences, so you can run it
during normal operation until the last delta is small. At the end it
checks each mailbox against the source it read and reports it as
.IR verified .
.PP
Mailboxes that the source no longer has are removed from the target
.RB ( \-no-prune
keeps them; INBOX always stays). Sieve scripts are copied when
.B \-sieve-addr
is given, except
.BR jmap-vacation :
a ShardPost source lists it, but it is the server's copy of the JMAP
vacation response, and the target refuses it.
.PP
While a run is in progress, the account is locked: IMAP, POP3 and LMTP
refuse it. Role:
.BR import .
.SS Public folders
The second form imports a namespace of the source other than the
personal one \(em a public namespace of Dovecot, as a rule \(em into the
public folders. The account is
.B ~public
(quote it: a shell expands the tilde),
.B \-source-user
is the source account whose view is read, and
.B \-source-namespace
is the prefix of the namespace there. The folders land below
.BR \-target-prefix ;
the namespace's own mailbox, if it has one
.RI ( Zabbix
of the namespace
.IR Zabbix/ ),
takes the name of the prefix. The identities are kept exactly as for an
account.
.PP
What differs from an account:
.IP \(bu 2
.B Rights are not copied.
A public namespace of Dovecot takes its rights from the configuration and
from groups, which have no counterpart here. Grant them with
.BR shardpostctl-acl (1);
an imported folder without an entry is visible to nobody.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \eSeen is private per account.
The messages carry the \eSeen of the source user, and that is what every
account without a state of its own starts from. For each account named
with
.B \-readers
the migrator logs in at the source as that account and records where its
\eSeen differs, and its subscriptions of these folders. A folder the
account does not see at the source is left alone. The source user itself
needs no entry for \eSeen, only for its subscriptions.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Pruning stays below the target prefix.
Public folders outside it are never removed, the folder the prefix itself
names stays, and without a prefix nothing is pruned.
.IP \(bu 2
.B No Sieve scripts.
They belong to accounts.
.PP
While such a run is in progress the public namespace is locked: a client
gets NO [UNAVAILABLE] for LIST and for every public folder, a session
that has a public folder selected is closed within a few seconds, and a
delivery that files into a public folder is deferred. The first copy of
a large namespace therefore belongs into a quiet hour.
.PP
Import into folders nobody uses here yet. A later run makes the target
what the source is: a reader's \eSeen becomes exactly the source's, so
what that account read here in the meantime is unread again, and the
reader marks of an earlier run have moved HIGHESTMODSEQ past the source,
so a client that synchronized such a folder here between two runs can
miss flag changes of the second. Grant the rights after the last run, or
keep clients away until then.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Source
.TP
.BI \-source-addr " host:port"
Source IMAP server. Required.
.TP
.BI \-source-tls " implicit|starttls|none"
How to secure the connection. Default:
.IR implicit .
.TP
.BI \-source-server-name " name"
Certificate name to expect. Default: the host of
.BR \-source-addr .
.TP
.BI \-source-ca " file"
CA for the source and for ManageSieve. Default: the system roots.
.TP
.B \-source-proxy
Send a PROXY v1 header to the source, for load balancers that require it.
.TP
.BI \-source-timeout " duration"
Timeout per source command. Default:
.IR 2m .
.SS Login
.TP
.BI \-source-user " name"
Login name at the source. Default: the account. Required for
.BR ~public .
.TP
.BI \-master-user " name"
Master user of the source. Without it, the migrator logs in as the user.
.TP
.BI \-user-format " format"
How the master login is composed. Default:
.IR {user}*{master} .
.TP
.BI \-password-file " file"
File holding the password of the (master) user. Required.
.SS Sieve
.TP
.BI \-sieve-addr " host:port"
ManageSieve of the source. Without it, Sieve is not copied.
.TP
.BI \-sieve-tls " implicit|starttls|none"
Default:
.IR starttls .
.TP
.BI \-sieve-server-name " name"
Certificate name to expect for ManageSieve.
.TP
.B \-sieve-proxy
Send a PROXY v1 header to ManageSieve.
.SS Public folders
.TP
.BI \-source-namespace " prefix"
Import this namespace of the source instead of the personal one, for
example
.IR Mailinglists/ .
The prefix is the one the source reports in NAMESPACE.
.TP
.BI \-target-prefix " name/"
Put this in front of the imported names. Empty, or a mailbox name ending
in a slash.
.TP
.BI \-readers " account,..."
Accounts whose own \eSeen and subscriptions are carried over. Only with
the account
.BR ~public .
Each of them is logged in at the source under its account name, with the
same master login; an account that is called something else there cannot
be a reader. A folder the account can list but not open is skipped with
a warning. A client with a scope needs the readers in it, not only
.BR ~public .
.SS Run
.TP
.BI \-session " id"
Resume this import session instead of opening a new one.
.TP
.B \-no-prune
Keep target mailboxes the source no longer has.
.TP
.B \-no-acl
Do not copy mailbox ACLs. A namespace import never copies them.
.TP
.B \-reimport
Replace target mailboxes whose UIDVALIDITY no longer matches the source.
Their messages are imported again.
.TP
.BI \-pop3-uidl-format " format"
The source's
.I pop3_uidl_format
(Dovecot syntax), so POP3 clients keep their UIDLs. Without it, POP3
reports the message GUID.
.TP
.BI \-batch-messages " n"
Messages per import request. Default:
.IR 200 .
.TP
.BI \-batch-bytes " n"
Bytes per import request; a batch ends at whichever limit comes first,
this one or
.BR \-batch-messages .
Default:
.IR 33554432
(32 MiB). The server has limits of its own
.RI ( admin.import.max_batch_bytes ", " admin.import.max_batch_messages )
and refuses a larger batch.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the report as JSON.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every flag of the source and of ManageSieve reads its default from the
environment, which is how the import sidecar is configured: the
deployment sets the variables once and the command is called without
flags. A flag on the command line wins.
.TP
.B SHARDPOST_MIGRATE_SOURCE_ADDR
Default of
.BR \-source-addr .
.TP
.B SHARDPOST_MIGRATE_SOURCE_TLS
Default of
.BR \-source-tls .
.TP
.B SHARDPOST_MIGRATE_SOURCE_SERVER_NAME
Default of
.BR \-source-server-name .
.TP
.B SHARDPOST_MIGRATE_SOURCE_CA
Default of
.BR \-source-ca .
.TP
.B SHARDPOST_MIGRATE_SOURCE_PROXY
Default of
.BR \-source-proxy .
.TP
.B SHARDPOST_MIGRATE_MASTER_USER
Default of
.BR \-master-user .
.TP
.B SHARDPOST_MIGRATE_USER_FORMAT
Default of
.BR \-user-format .
.TP
.B SHARDPOST_MIGRATE_PASSWORD_FILE
Default of
.BR \-password-file .
.TP
.B SHARDPOST_MIGRATE_SIEVE_ADDR
Default of
.BR \-sieve-addr .
.TP
.B SHARDPOST_MIGRATE_SIEVE_TLS
Default of
.BR \-sieve-tls .
.TP
.B SHARDPOST_MIGRATE_SIEVE_SERVER_NAME
Default of
.BR \-sieve-server-name .
.TP
.B SHARDPOST_MIGRATE_SIEVE_PROXY
Default of
.BR \-sieve-proxy .
.TP
.B SHARDPOST_MIGRATE_POP3_UIDL_FORMAT
Default of
.BR \-pop3-uidl-format .
.PP
The two proxy variables are read as the literal string
.IR true ;
anything else counts as off.
.PP
The connection to this server is configured as for every other command,
see
.BR shardpostctl (1).
.SH EXAMPLES
A first copy while the account still lives on the old server. Repeat it
until a run reports few changes:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl -timeout 10m migrate \\
    -source-addr mail.example.org:993 \\
    -master-user admin -password-file /run/secrets/master \\
    -sieve-addr mail.example.org:4190 \\
    user@example.org
.RE
.fi
.PP
In the cluster the sidecar already carries the settings:
.PP
.nf
.RS
kubectl -n shardpost exec deploy/shardpost-server -c import -- \\
    shardpostctl -timeout 10m migrate user@example.org
.RE
.fi
.PP
A long run, detached, with its log on the work volume:
.PP
.nf
.RS
kubectl -n shardpost exec deploy/shardpost-server -c import -- sh -c \\
    'nohup shardpostctl -timeout 10m migrate --json user@example.org \\
     >/work/user.json 2>/work/user.log &'
.RE
.fi
.PP
Keep POP3 UIDLs stable for a Dovecot 2.3 source:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl migrate -pop3-uidl-format '%08Xu%08Xv' \\
    -source-addr mail.example.org:993 \\
    -master-user admin -password-file /run/secrets/master \\
    user@example.org
.RE
.fi
.PP
The public namespace
.I Mailinglists/
of a Dovecot server, as lists@example.org sees it, into
.IR Public/Mailinglists/... ,
with the reading state of a second account; then the rights, which the
import does not copy:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl -timeout 10m migrate \\
    -source-user lists@example.org \\
    -source-namespace Mailinglists/ -target-prefix Mailinglists/ \\
    -readers lists@example.org,user@example.org '~public'
shardpostctl acl set '~public' Mailinglists/Dovecot \\
    user@example.org lrswipkxte
.RE
.fi
.PP
A namespace that is a single mailbox
.RI ( Zabbix/
with the mailbox
.IR Zabbix )
becomes the folder the prefix names:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl migrate -source-user lists@example.org \\
    -source-namespace Zabbix/ -target-prefix Zabbix/ '~public'
.RE
.fi
.PP
The cutover, once the copy is close: hold the account, kick its sessions,
run the last delta, then move it.
.PP
.nf
.RS
nauthilus-directorctl users hold set user@example.org --duration 15m \\
    --reason mailbox-move
nauthilus-directorctl users kick user@example.org --reason mailbox-move
shardpostctl migrate user@example.org            # all mailboxes verified
nauthilus-directorctl users move user@example.org \\
    --to-shard shardpost --strategy kick_existing
nauthilus-directorctl users hold clear user@example.org --reason done
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything was copied and verified.
.TP
.B 1
Parts failed. Run the command again; it continues where it left off.
.TP
.B 64
Usage error, for example a missing
.B \-source-addr
or
.BR \-password-file ,
or options of a namespace import that do not fit together.
.TP
.B 69
The run was aborted.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR shardpostctl (1),
.BR shardpostctl-export (1),
.BR shardpostctl-acl (1),
.BR shardpostctl-account (1)
.PP
The import semantics and the migration procedure are described in
.IR docs/admin-api.md .
